<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chap-differentation"  xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Integration and Differentiation</title>
<introduction>

<blockquote>
<p><em><q>Mathematics we can compare with a high ridge of mountain, to which lead steep alleys, and stepping on them sometimes requires a great cost of hard work and toil.</q></em></p>
<attribution>
Otto M. Nikodym
</attribution>
</blockquote>

<p>In this chapter we will answer a question that has loomed over us since the beginning of these notes: The Fundamental Theorem(s) of Calculus. If <m>f</m> is a continuous function on <m>[a,b]</m>, when can we be guaranteed that
<md> f(b) - f(a) = \int_{[a,b]}f'\,dm?</md>
To answer this we must first answer: when is <m>f'</m> defined and integrable?</p>
<p>We begin with some general measure theoretic tools: the Radon-Nikodym Theorem and the Lebesgue Decomposition Theorem. We will then introduce absolutely continuous functions on the closed intervals of the real line.
    These will be the functions for which the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus (for Lebesgue integration) holds.
    We end this chapter with the Lebesgue decomposition of functions on the real line.
</p>
</introduction>

